{
  "id": "crusader",
  "name": "Operation Crusader",
  "about": "The relief of Tobruk, 18 November to 30 December 1941. Each division is shown as the units that fought within it, under its HQ; units of one division that start in one hex start as a group. Formations from the published orders of battle (docs/SOURCES.md). Each unit's strength is its figure on the first day with a source letter (see sources). Positions are approximate, to a hex or two. Lift, air points, sea_pct and the opening stocks are first guesses, to be tuned. Arrivals, withdrawals and replacements fall on their historical dates, each with a source letter; where a date or a number is worked out and not stated by the source, its basis says so. Left out: Oasis Force at Jarabub.",
  "sources": {
    "W": "Wikipedia, Operation Crusader: 7th Armoured Brigade, 129 tanks",
    "H": "historyofwar.org, Operation Crusader: 4th Armoured Brigade 166 Stuarts; 22nd Armoured Brigade 155; 1st Army Tank Brigade about 130; 249 German and 189 Italian medium tanks in all",
    "E": "worked out from the unit's establishment, not from a strength return: about 800 men to a battalion, 12 guns to a battery group, 15 cars to a squadron",
    "M": "from memory and unverified: the split of the German 249 between the two panzer regiments, Ariete's 146 of the Italian 189, and 32nd Army Tank Brigade's 100",
    "N": "W. E. Murphy, The Relief of Tobruk (New Zealand official history, 1961): the New Zealand Division's return to Egypt on the night of 1 December 1941 without 5th Brigade and the Divisional Cavalry; 4th Armoured Brigade's tanks, 37 on the night of 24 November, 70 on the 27th, 98 on 4 December, 136 on the 6th; the Afrika Korps with 70 tanks on 28 December",
    "P": "R. Palmer, 7 Armoured Division (1941-42), britishmilitaryhistory.co.uk, 2020: on 27 November 1941 what was left of 7th Armoured Brigade came under GHQ Middle East Forces",
    "S": "Wikipedia, 2nd South African Infantry Division: it guarded the railway and the coast behind the army until it took over the frontier area on 2 December 1941",
    "J": "Wikipedia, 22nd Armoured Brigade (United Kingdom), citing Joslen pp. 168-9: 80 Crusaders and 30 Stuarts by 22 December 1941",
    "R": "rommelsriposte.com (the Crusader Project), from shipping returns: on 19 December 1941 the Ankara landed 22 tanks at Benghazi and a second ship 23 at Tripoli, both lots for Panzer-Regiment 8"
  },
